{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|da|en}} {{term|Karen|lang=da}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈkaɹən/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈkæɹən/|/ˈkɛɹən/|/ˈkɑɹən/|/ˈkɛɚn/|/ˈkɑɚn/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|æɹən|ɛɹən|ɛə(ɹ)n|ɑː(ɹ)n|lang=en}}
* {{homophones|lang=en|caring|cairn|carn}} {{qualifier|some American accents}}

====Proper noun====
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{given name|female|from=Ancient Greek}}. First taken up as a given name in the U.S.A., and popular in the English-speaking world from the 1950s to the 1970s.
#* '''1878''' [[w:Celia Thaxter|Celia Thaxter]], ''Drift-Weed'', Houghton, Osgood,1878, page 28 ("Karen"):
#*: Left you a lover in that far land, / O '''Karen''' sad, that you pine so long! / Would I could unravel and understand / That sorrowful, sweet Norwegian song!
#* '''1918''' Cecily Ullman Sidgwick, ''Karen'', W.Collins, 1918, page 12:
#*: I was not called '''Karen''' after Hans Andersen's dancing girl, but after a Danish friend of my mother's who married an Englishman and was my godmother. So much for our family affairs.

===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|xpr|en}} {{m|xpr|𐭊𐭓𐭍𐭉|tr=krny /Kārēn/}}. The Armenian name is from {{etyl|hy|en}} {{m|hy|Կարեն}}, from the same Parthian name.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|Garen}} {{qualifier|from Western Armenian}}

====Proper noun====
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{lb|en|historical}} one of the seven great Parthian feudal families
# {{given name|male|from=Armenian|A=An Armenian|lang=en}}

